  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Graphs are the most versatile data structure in computer science. They model anything that involves connections cities and roads, friends on a social network, web pages and links.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-is-a-graph",
      "title": "1. What is a Graph?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A graph is made up of two things:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong>Nodes</strong>&nbsp;(also called vertices) the things you're modelling. Cities, people, web pages.",
            "<strong>Edges</strong>&nbsp;the connections between them. Roads, friendships, hyperlinks."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's it. Any system where things connect to other things can be modelled as a graph.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-623086696.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-623086696.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-623086696.png",
          "alt": "What is a Graph?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5 nodes (A, B, C, D, E) connected by 6 edges. Simple but this structure can model enormous real-world systems.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-302986719.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-302986719.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852584-302986719.png",
          "alt": "What is a Graph?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Undirected Graph:&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Edges go both ways if&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A connects to B</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, then&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">B also connects to A</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;(like mutual friendship).</span>",
            "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Directed Graph:&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Edges have direction&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A → B</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;doesn’t mean&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">B → A</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;(like following someone on Twitter).</span>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-370516127.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-370516127.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-370516127.png",
          "alt": "What is a Graph?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Graphs can also be&nbsp;<strong>weighted&nbsp;</strong>each edge carries a number (distance, cost, time). A road map with distances is a weighted undirected graph. A flight network with ticket prices is a weighted directed graph.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-276641647.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-276641647.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785405852835-276641647.png",
          "alt": "What is a Graph?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-how-to-represent-a-graph-in-code",
      "title": "2. How to Represent a Graph in Code",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two ways. The right one depends on how dense your graph is.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Adjacency List &nbsp;(<span style=\"font-size: 1.75rem; letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">most common</span>)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each node stores a list of its neighbours. Space efficient only stores edges that actually exist. Used for most interview problems.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Adjacency list</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n\n    unordered_map<string, vector<string>> graph = {\n        {\"A\", {\"B\", \"C\"}},\n        {\"B\", {\"A\", \"D\"}},\n        {\"C\", {\"A\", \"D\", \"E\"}},\n        {\"D\", {\"B\", \"C\"}},\n        {\"E\", {\"C\"}}\n    };\n\n    // check neighbours of A\n    for (string neighbour : graph[\"A\"]) {\n        cout << neighbour << \" \";\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "B C"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Adjacency Matrix ( For dense graphs )",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 2D grid where&nbsp;<code>matrix[i][j] = 1</code>&nbsp;means there's an edge from node i to node j. Fast to check if an edge exists, but uses O(V²) space even for sparse graphs.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Adjacency matrix (4 nodes: 0,1,2,3)</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n\n    vector<vector<int>> matrix = {\n        {0, 1, 1, 0},  // node 0 connects to 1, 2\n        {1, 0, 0, 1},  // node 1 connects to 0, 3\n        {1, 0, 0, 1},  // node 2 connects to 0, 3\n        {0, 1, 1, 0}   // node 3 connects to 1, 2\n    };\n\n    // check if edge exists between 0 and 2\n    cout << matrix[0][2];  // 1 → yes\n\n    return 0;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "1"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When implementing a graph, you first&nbsp;<strong>choose a representation: Adjacency List or Adjacency Matrix</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948277-200994244.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Adjacency List</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;stores neighbors of each vertex, uses&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(V + E) space</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, and is best for&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">sparse graphs (most interview problems)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Adjacency Matrix</strong>&nbsp;uses a&nbsp;<strong>V × V matrix</strong>, takes&nbsp;<strong>O(V²) space</strong>, but allows&nbsp;<strong>O(1) edge lookup</strong>&nbsp;and works well for&nbsp;<strong>dense graphs</strong>.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-key-graph-terms-you-must-know",
      "title": "3. Key Graph Terms You Must Know",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before starting Graph problems in DSA, you should understand these basic graph terms because they appear in almost every graph problem.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Degree&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The number of edges connected to a node. In the example below, node A has degree <b>3 because 3 edges</b> connect to it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948277-717591383.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. In-Degree / Out-Degree",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Only in <b>directed</b> graphs.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">In-degree = edges coming INTO a node.&nbsp;</span><br><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Out-degree = edges going OUT.&nbsp;</span><br><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Node A has out-degree 2, in-degree 0.&nbsp;</span><br><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Node D has in-degree 2, out-degree 0.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948277-909803441.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "3. Path",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A sequence of nodes where each consecutive pair is connected by an edge. A→B→C→D is a path of length 3 you visit 4 nodes crossing 3 edges.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948278-384802132.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "4. Cycle",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "&nbsp;A path that starts and ends at the same node.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A→B→C→A is a cycle.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Detecting cycles is a very common interview problem DAGs are specifically graphs with NO cycles.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948278-959171116.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "5. Connected vs Disconnected",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A graph is connected if you can reach every node from any other node. If some nodes are isolated in a separate group, those are called <b>separate components.</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<br><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948278-961335943.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "6. DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A directed graph with absolutely no cycles edges only flow forward, never loop back. Used heavily in task scheduling, build systems, and dependency resolution.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948544-10072828.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "7. Weight",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A number on an edge representing cost, distance, or time.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Algorithms like Dijkstra's use weights </b>to find the shortest/cheapest path between nodes.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785405948545-471484011.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-common-operations",
