          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "Where Graphs Show Up",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<b>Social networks</b> people as nodes, friendships as edges",
            "<b>Maps and navigation</b> cities as nodes, roads as weighted edges",
            "<b>Web crawling</b> pages as nodes, hyperlinks as directed edges",
            "<b>Task scheduling</b> dependencies between tasks (DAG)",
            "<b>Network </b>routing finding the fastest path between servers",
            "<b>Recommendation systems </b>users and products as connected nodes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Coming up next:&nbsp;</strong>Now that you understand the structure, the next chapters cover how to&nbsp;<strong>traverse&nbsp;</strong>a graph BFS (Breadth First Search) and DFS (Depth First Search) which are the foundation for almost every graph algorithm.</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
